<Page
  title="About Patriotic Angler"
  description="Who runs Patriotic Angler, how we pick gear, and what our Made in USA stamps mean."
  lede="Patriotic Angler is a small, independent fishing site for beginners, families and weekend anglers."
>
  <p>
    We write the guides we wish we'd had starting out: how to tie a knot that holds, how to rig a worm, where the
    bluegill are in July, and what to buy without overspending. Each post tries to answer one question well, in plain
    words, so you can read it in the truck and go fish.
  </p>

  <h2>How we pick gear</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>It has to be something a beginner or weekend angler will actually use.</li>
    <li>It has to have a long track record with anglers. We look at how widely it's used and how buyers rate it before we recommend it.</li>
    <li>It has to be easy to find, so you're not chasing a lure no store carries.</li>
    <li>Nobody pays to be included. If that ever changes, we'll say so on the page.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What the stamps mean</h2>
  <p>
    American-made gear gets a brass stamp. <strong>Made in USA</strong> means the maker or the product listing says the
    product is made in the USA. <strong>Assembled in USA</strong> means it's put together here from parts made somewhere
    else. If nobody says, or the labels disagree, it gets no stamp. The full story is in our
    <a href="/american-made-fishing-gear-guide/">American-made gear guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How the site makes money</h2>
  <p>
    Some product buttons go to Amazon. If you buy something after clicking one, Amazon pays us a small commission. The
    price is the same for you. <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">Read the full disclosure</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Get in touch</h2>
  <p>Spotted a mistake, or want a topic covered? <a href="/contact/">Send us a note</a>.</p>
</Page>
